                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "[h2]\"Thanks for playing, friendo! You're a kind person! :) But 'your' quest has only just begun, questie! The world is in your paws! So make sure to spread your kindness like a wildfire!\"[/h2]\r\n\r\nHonestly, I was anything but ready for what [b]Furquest[/b] would be. What was I expecting? EarthBound meets WarioWare, quirky, funny, lighthearted, right? But what did I get? Calling it just EarthBound meets WarioWare is just unfair in hindsight, with an experience that amounted so much more than just that. I don't even know where I could start, really.\r\n\r\nBy spending time to actually backtrack, interact, take everything in, what I can assure you is that the game's Steam page, and the actual experience are vastly different. The actual game, especially in times such as these, is just a ray of sunshine (mostly). It's quirky, wholesome, encouraging, supportive, random, chaotic, unapologetically weird and loveable, to the point where it makes me feel like the game was built around \"[i]I may be cringe, but I am free.[/i]\"\r\n\r\nIs the game perfect? No. And? And what. Simply reducing all titles to \"Oh it's X\" or \"it's just Y\", or in this case, EarthBound or UT/DR, sets incorrect expectations, and may even set one's self up to not allow themselves to enjoy it, simply due to comparing, even if just subconsciously. [b]Furquest[/b] is doing so much, from artstyle, gameplay, characters, worldbuilding, story, everything, that it genuinely feels like it's own identity, so allow yourself to experience it as such. <3\r\n\r\nThe end though? Both endings are depressing, really. And yeah, the game is a ray of sunshine, but there'll also be rain. I'm not gonna spoil it or go too in-depth into it, but I feel like this quote from in-game covers it well: \"[i]I won't ask you to forgive me. [...] I know none of this is fair of me to ask of you... But when has life ever been fair? ... The choice is yours.[/i]\"\r\n\r\nAll in all, you know what I loved most about it? There's so much centered around love of one's self and others, with so much support and encouragement, but there's no toxic positivity or just delusory mindset. The game is there for us, and not just for us to experience it, but also to support us, even when things are bad, and it's up to us to take everything in, make our decisions, and keep our heads up. [b]Furquest[/b] is just that heavily neurodivergent friend that won't take away the bad, but will support you through it, and while it won't know what exactly it needs to tell every single one of us, there will be one or more things which will be just what we needed to be told.\r\n\r\nWhat were my issues with it? I wish there'd be more and longer soundtracks, because at some points it simply starts to feel too repetitive given how short they can be. Also, I wish that there'd be consistent \"fast travel\" companions across all areas, because I don't mind backtracking, and all of them really are fun to interact with, but their locations and travelling points feel inconsistent, which ends up feeling unreliable. That's really it. These are literally my biggest issues with the game.\r\n\r\nThere's still plenty I haven't mentioned, from the characters, to the minigames, the artstyle, worldbuilding, and so on, but I've already rambled a lot. So if you're looking for a quirky, wholesome, supportive, funny, unapologetically weird and serious at times lil' game, you gotta pick it up!",
